# Citation metadata of skforecast (https://citation-file-format.github.io).
# GitHub builds its "Cite this repository" button from this file, and Zenodo
# takes the metadata of each release (description, keywords, authors) from it.
# There is no `version` or `date-released` on purpose, as in scikit-learn or
# pandas: Zenodo takes both from each GitHub release, and the version to cite
# is the one listed on Zenodo. If they are ever added back, keep them current:
# `python tools/ai/generate_ai_context_files.py --check` verifies the version.

cff-version: 1.2.0
title: skforecast
message: >-
  If you use skforecast in a scientific publication, please cite the version
  you used. The DOI below always resolves to the latest release, and the DOI
  of each version is listed on Zenodo.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Joaquin
    family-names: Amat Rodrigo
    email: j.amatrodrigo@gmail.com
    orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0009-0001-9531-9828'
  - given-names: Javier
    family-names: Escobar Ortiz
    email: javier.escobar.ortiz@gmail.com
    orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0009-0005-4399-7634'
repository-code: 'https://github.com/skforecast/skforecast'
url: 'https://skforecast.org/'
abstract: >-
  skforecast is a Python library for time series forecasting using
  scikit-learn compatible models, statistical methods, and foundation models.
  It covers single and multiple series, exogenous variables, backtesting,
  hyperparameter tuning and probabilistic forecasting.
keywords:
  - time series
  - forecasting
  - time series forecasting
  - machine learning
  - scikit-learn
  - statistical models
  - foundation models
  - python
license: BSD-3-Clause
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.8382787
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.8382787
    description: Concept DOI of skforecast, resolves to the latest release.
